1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,350
Mrs.

2
00:00:00,931 --> 00:00:03,903
Rao stands in front of
a shelf full of snacks.

3
00:00:04,203 --> 00:00:06,976
Biscuits, sweets, chips and protein bars.

4
00:00:07,526 --> 00:00:09,454
Why should she pick Laddoo Lab?

5
00:00:09,454 --> 00:00:11,658
She has about two seconds to decide.

6
00:00:12,308 --> 00:00:14,566
Arjun sees it on every shop visit.

7
00:00:14,566 --> 00:00:16,375
The snack shelf is crowded.

8
00:00:16,725 --> 00:00:19,156
Busy parents are Laddoo Lab's target.

9
00:00:19,156 --> 00:00:21,961
But many brands want the same tiffin box.

10
00:00:22,311 --> 00:00:24,087
So Zoya asks a new question.

11
00:00:24,087 --> 00:00:27,215
When parents hear Laddoo
Lab, what should they think?

12
00:00:27,865 --> 00:00:30,405
That question is about market positioning.

13
00:00:30,705 --> 00:00:35,196
Kotler and Armstrong say a product's
position is the place it holds in

14
00:00:35,196 --> 00:00:38,564
customers' minds, compared
with competing products.

15
00:00:38,914 --> 00:00:42,508
The word was made famous
by Al Ries and Jack Trout.

16
00:00:42,508 --> 00:00:47,359
Their book, Positioning: The Battle
for Your Mind, came out in 1981.

17
00:00:47,709 --> 00:00:52,236
In simple words: positioning is the
one idea you want to own in the buyer's

18
00:00:52,236 --> 00:00:53,136
mind.

19
00:00:53,419 --> 00:00:55,895
Let's watch Zoya draw a positioning map.

20
00:00:56,245 --> 00:00:57,804
She draws two lines.

21
00:00:57,804 --> 00:00:59,353
Indulgent or healthy.

22
00:00:59,353 --> 00:01:01,614
And easy for tiffins, or not.

23
00:01:01,964 --> 00:01:05,266
Cream biscuits are easy to
pack, but not very healthy.

24
00:01:05,616 --> 00:01:09,814
Sweet shop mithai is rich and
festive, but messy in a tiffin box.

25
00:01:10,164 --> 00:01:13,737
Protein bars are healthy, but
made for adults at the gym.

26
00:01:14,087 --> 00:01:16,288
Now look at the top right corner.

27
00:01:16,288 --> 00:01:18,329
Healthy, and made for tiffins.

28
00:01:18,329 --> 00:01:19,496
Nobody is there.

29
00:01:19,846 --> 00:01:22,400
That empty spot is Laddoo Lab's chance.

30
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:24,454
So the position is clear.

31
00:01:24,454 --> 00:01:26,689
The healthy sweet for tiffin boxes.

32
00:01:27,339 --> 00:01:29,486
Don't fight for a crowded spot.

33
00:01:29,486 --> 00:01:30,850
Own an empty one.

34
00:01:31,550 --> 00:01:35,714
Kotler and Armstrong give a simple
template for a positioning statement.

35
00:01:36,064 --> 00:01:38,113
To, the target and their need.

36
00:01:38,113 --> 00:01:40,875
Busy parents who want
a healthy tiffin snack.

37
00:01:41,225 --> 00:01:43,428
Our brand is, the big idea.

38
00:01:43,428 --> 00:01:46,893
Laddoo Lab is the healthy
sweet for tiffin boxes.

39
00:01:47,243 --> 00:01:49,213
That, the point of difference.

40
00:01:49,213 --> 00:01:52,274
Made with ragi and jaggery,
and no refined sugar.

41
00:01:52,924 --> 00:01:55,609
But not every difference
is worth shouting about.

42
00:01:55,959 --> 00:02:00,061
A good difference must matter to
buyers, be different from rivals, and

43
00:02:00,061 --> 00:02:01,382
be easy to explain.

44
00:02:01,732 --> 00:02:02,852
A round shape?

45
00:02:02,852 --> 00:02:05,599
Nobody cares, and every laddoo is round.

46
00:02:05,949 --> 00:02:07,149
No refined sugar?

47
00:02:07,149 --> 00:02:11,360
Parents care a lot, rivals can't
say it, and it fits on one line.

48
00:02:12,010 --> 00:02:14,432
Where do we see positioning at work today?

49
00:02:14,782 --> 00:02:15,682
Attribute.

50
00:02:15,604 --> 00:02:16,649
What it has.

51
00:02:16,649 --> 00:02:19,887
A phone maker builds its
position on the camera.

52
00:02:20,237 --> 00:02:21,137
Benefit.

53
00:02:20,966 --> 00:02:22,610
What it does for you.

54
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:25,139
An airline promises the lowest fares.

55
00:02:25,489 --> 00:02:26,389
User.

56
00:02:26,096 --> 00:02:27,388
Who it is for.

57
00:02:27,388 --> 00:02:30,850
A bank offers an account
made just for students.

58
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:32,100
Occasion.

59
00:02:31,972 --> 00:02:33,282
When you use it.

60
00:02:33,282 --> 00:02:36,522
A bakery is known for
fresh bread every morning.

61
00:02:37,172 --> 00:02:38,971
But can positioning go wrong?

62
00:02:39,321 --> 00:02:40,406
The good side.

63
00:02:40,406 --> 00:02:44,843
A clear position makes choosing easy,
and lifts you above price fights.

64
00:02:45,193 --> 00:02:46,328
The hard side.

65
00:02:46,328 --> 00:02:48,087
A position is a promise.

66
00:02:48,087 --> 00:02:50,357
Break it, and trust breaks too.

67
00:02:50,707 --> 00:02:54,014
Dev wants to add sugar
sprinkles, to look more fun.

68
00:02:54,364 --> 00:02:55,364
Nani says no.

69
00:02:55,364 --> 00:02:57,549
It would break the healthy promise.

70
00:02:57,899 --> 00:03:00,258
Too many claims also confuse people.

71
00:03:00,258 --> 00:03:03,263
Healthy, cheap, festive
and full of protein?

72
00:03:03,263 --> 00:03:04,712
Nobody believes that.

73
00:03:05,062 --> 00:03:05,962
The lesson?

74
00:03:05,903 --> 00:03:09,192
Pick one clear promise, and
keep it every single day.

75
00:03:09,842 --> 00:03:10,801
Quick check.

76
00:03:10,801 --> 00:03:14,767
Zoya says, Laddoo Lab is the
healthy sweet for tiffin boxes.

77
00:03:14,767 --> 00:03:16,498
Which step is she doing?

78
00:03:18,398 --> 00:03:19,298
Positioning!

79
00:03:19,296 --> 00:03:23,153
She is choosing the place Laddoo
Lab holds in parents' minds.

80
00:03:23,753 --> 00:03:24,762
So, remember.

81
00:03:25,062 --> 00:03:29,051
Positioning is the place your
brand holds in the buyer's mind.

82
00:03:29,401 --> 00:03:33,272
A positioning map shows the
crowded spots, and the empty ones.

83
00:03:33,622 --> 00:03:36,565
Pick one difference that
matters, and keep the promise.

84
00:03:37,165 --> 00:03:40,745
Next time, we'll put it all
together in the STP framework.
